From: ROG5 native Linux project
Subject: [PATCH] media: iris: release OEM VP9 scratch registrations before restart

STOP/RELEASE_RESOURCES leaves the input scratch buffers registered and
marked QUEUED. A second OUTPUT STREAMON skips destroying them but creates
and registers another set. On SM8350 VP9 the new SCRATCH address supersedes
the old one; the next source change releases the old address and asserts
in vpxDec_SessionReleaseBuffers, vpx_decoder.c:1132.

Use the existing release/wait/allocate/queue path on OEM VP9 input streamon,
just as on source change. Stock msm_comm_set_scratch_buffers unregisters
old scratch buffers before registering replacements, including on restart.
Leave persist buffers, DPBs and other codecs unchanged.

---
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_vdec.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_vdec.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_vdec.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_vdec.c
@@ -373,6 +373,14 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	/* OEM VP9 keeps one firmware registration per scratch type across STOP. */
+	if (inst->core->iris_platform_data->oem && inst->codec == V4L2_PIX_FMT_VP9) {
+		ret = iris_alloc_and_queue_input_int_bufs(inst);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+		goto start;
+	}
+
 	iris_get_internal_buffers(inst, V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_OUTPUT_MPLANE);
 
 	ret = iris_destroy_dequeued_internal_buffers(inst, V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_OUTPUT_MPLANE);
@@ -387,6 +395,7 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+start:
 	inst->frame_counter = 0;
 
 	return iris_process_streamon_input(inst);
